{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall surely return to him the pledge when the sun comes in, etc.</b> If it is a night garment, etc. (Bava Metzi'a 113b), but a day garment was mentioned in the portion of Mishpatim (Exodus 22:25), as it says: ״עַד בֹּא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ תְּשִׁיבֶנּוּ לוֹ״. And by way of allusion, He warns the rich who instruct their heirs that after their death they should return the pledges to the poor, and for this they receive no reward at all, for then the money is not theirs. And furthermore, at that time they are exempt from the commandments, and the dead are free of them; therefore He warns them ״עַד בֹּא הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ — before the setting of your sun, ״תְּשִׁיבֶנּוּ לוֹ״. And here He said ״כְּבוֹא״ with a כַ״ף, meaning close to the setting of your sun and before it you shall return it to him, while you are still subject to obligation and while the pledge is still yours. And in the portion of Mishpatim we spoke at length about this." "And according to its plain sense, He doubled ״הָשֵׁב תָּשִׁיב״ to say that each and every day he shall return it to him. And if you say: if so, that defeats the purpose — why do I need it, if every night I return it to him and I am nothing but his guardian by day? — about this He said: ״וּלְךָ תִּהְיֶה צְדָקָה לִפְנֵי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״, for it is considered before HaShem your Elohim as though each and every day you are giving him a new charity. And this is a proof that a creditor acquires the pledge, for otherwise, whence charity? (Pesachim 31b). And according to the law there is a practical difference in the matter, namely that the poor man does not bequeath the pledge to his sons if they were rich. And in saying ״וּלְךָ תִּהְיֶה צְדָקָה״ He diminishes the heirs, for if you do not return it until after the setting of your sun in this world, then the seed for charity will not be yours but rather your heirs', and if you return it during the time of obligation in your lifetime then it will be yours, according to the allusion that we mentioned." "And according to its plain sense, this verse teaches a precious principle, and it is like the saying of Munbaz the King who scattered his treasures, etc.: 'My fathers stored up for others and I stored up for myself,' as it says: ״וּלְךָ תִּהְיֶה צְדָקָה״; 'My fathers stored up in a place where the hand reaches, and I stored up in a place where the hand does not reach,' as it says (Psalms 89:15): ״צֶדֶק וּמִשְׁפָּט מְכוֹן כִּסְאֶךָ״ (Bava Batra 11a). And the one depends on the other, for since charity is placed before HaShem as the established foundation of His throne, therefore it is yours and not for strangers, for no person's hand reaches there. Therefore it says: ״וּלְךָ תִּהְיֶה צְדָקָה״, and not for others. And He gave a reason for the matter, for charity is placed before HaShem your Elohim as the established foundation of His throne, and who would ascend heavenward to take it from your hand?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:47.481867242Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}